<Page title={`About ${SITE_NAME}`} description={`Who runs ${SITE_NAME}, what we cover, and how we stay independent.`}>
  <p>{SITE_NAME} helps homeowners decide how to keep the power on during an outage, and what that will really cost: standby generators, portable generators, home batteries and portable power stations, plus the transfer switches and wiring that connect them to a house.</p>
  <h2>Why this site exists</h2>
  <p>Most backup power content is written by companies selling one answer. Generator dealers sell generators, solar installers sell batteries, and power station brands publish runtime claims for their own products. We compare the options side by side using published cost data, manufacturer specifications and safety agency guidance, and we show the math so you can check it.</p>
  <h2>Who runs this site</h2>
  <p>{SITE_NAME} is run by <strong>{AUTHOR}</strong>, the pen name of the site's owner. He is not an electrician and does not sell or install equipment. He has not tested the products discussed here, and the site never says otherwise. Every guide is research: cost guides from home-services sites, manufacturer spec sheets and manuals, U.S. safety agencies such as the CPSC and CDC, and owner reports gathered across many reviews.</p>
  <p>Anything that connects to your home's wiring must be installed by a licensed electrician, usually with a permit. Our guides explain the choices; they do not replace one.</p>
  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>We may earn a commission when you buy through some links, and we may be paid when we refer you to an installer through our <a href="/find-an-installer/">find an installer</a> form. Neither changes what we recommend. Details are on our <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">disclosure</a> page, and our methods are on <a href="/how-we-research/">how we research</a>.</p>
</Page>
